  "doc.title": "How Git Stores All History · A Hands-on Essay",
  "doc.desc": "Every time you run git add and git commit, Git does not store a stack of patches inside .git/—it writes an entire directory snapshot as immutable, content-addressed objects. Following Git 2.56.0 byte formats and primary documentation, this essay traces one commit all the way down: the .git/ map, blob headers and SHA-1/SHA-256, binary tree sorting and subtree sharing, the commit DAG, the index stat cache and Racy Git, refs, HEAD and reflog reachability, Myers diff and 3-way merge, packfile COPY/INSERT delta opcodes and fanout indices, wire protocol v2 and partial clones, and the reftable/SHA-256/sparse-index frontier. Nine hands-on models run the real algorithms rewritten in the page and crosschecked item by item against git 2.56.0.",
