  "hero.title": "How Git Stores All History",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Software principles · A hands-on long essay",
  "hero.sub": "Every time you run `git commit`, Git stores a complete snapshot of your entire project.<br>If every commit saves a full snapshot, why does disk usage not explode—and what actually changes when you amend or undo history?",
  "hero.lead": "Most people instinctively picture Git as a chronological chain of \"code patches\"—each commit recording only the handful of lines changed since the last one. Open `.git/` once and you discover the exact opposite: <strong>at its core, Git is not a diff-patch tool at all, but an immutable, content-addressed object database paired with a few mutable pointer files</strong>. This essay asks one question: <strong>what bytes does Git actually write into `.git/` when you run `git add` and `git commit`, why can those snapshots compare in milliseconds without blowing up disk space, and how can `git reflog` resurrect commits erased by `git reset --hard`?</strong>",
  "hero.note": "All byte layouts, algorithms, and frontier status notes here match <strong>Git 2.56.0</strong> (`/opt/homebrew/bin/git`, released 28 September 2026) and its shipped primary documentation (`gitformat-loose`, `gitformat-index`, `gitformat-pack`, `reftable`, `racy-git`, `BreakingChanges`). The page runs no external libraries: nine interactive models rewrite SHA-1, SHA-256, zlib DEFLATE compression, binary tree encoding, Myers diff, 3-way merge, and packfile delta opcodes from scratch in `models.js`, while `tools/crosscheck.cjs` checks 58 outputs directly against real `git 2.56.0` on disk and agrees on all 58. For background, see <a href=\"../../systems/a-secret-in-public/#ch6\">A Secret Agreed in Public, Chapter 6</a> (cryptographic hashes and avalanche) and <a href=\"../../algorithms/graph-algorithms/#step0\">Graph Algorithms</a> (graphs and traversal). This essay was written with an AI; the last paragraph of each chapter states what the model leaves out.",
  "ch1.title": "A map of `.git/`: the four regions",
  "toc.ch1": "Four regions of .git/",
  "ch1.law": "When you run `git add` and `git commit`, data flows through the working tree, the staging index (`.git/index`), and the object store (`.git/objects/`); all that moves at the very end is a 41-byte pointer file (`HEAD` and `refs/heads/`).",
  "ch1.p1": "Suppose you initialize an empty folder, create `hello.txt`, and run the four commands below. In the terminal you see only a two-line summary, yet on disk Git divides the repository into four cleanly separated regions.",
  "ch1.code.aria": "Minimal Git repository initialization and commit sequence",
  "ch1.code": "$ git init -b main\n$ printf 'hello git\\n' > hello.txt\n$ git add hello.txt\n$ git commit -m \"init\"",
  "ch1.p2": "The first region is the <strong>Working Tree</strong>—ordinary files and directories on disk, with zero history inside them. The second is the binary file `.git/index` (<strong>the staging area / dircache</strong>), holding the sorted manifest for the next snapshot together with each file's OS `stat` fingerprint. The third is `.git/objects/` (<strong>the object database</strong>): every committed file payload, directory snapshot, commit record, and annotated tag written here is immutable and addressed solely by the cryptographic hash of its bytes. The fourth consists of `HEAD` and `.git/refs/`, which store no project data at all—only <strong>rewritable sticky notes</strong> pointing to commits.",
  "dg.gitmap.cap": "Figure 1. The four regions of a Git repository. From top to bottom, `git add` hashes working files into objects and updates the index; `git commit` writes tree and commit objects and advances the branch pointer.",
  "dg.gitmap.aria": "Diagram: four layers — Working Tree, .git/index, .git/objects/, and HEAD/refs — with command flows between them",
  "dg.gitmap.l1": "Working Tree",
  "dg.gitmap.l1s": "Plain files and folders on disk",
  "dg.gitmap.l2": "Staging Index (.git/index)",
  "dg.gitmap.l2s": "Next snapshot manifest and stat cache",
  "dg.gitmap.l3": "Object Store (.git/objects/)",
  "dg.gitmap.l3s": "Immutable blobs, trees, commits, tags",
  "dg.gitmap.l4": "References (HEAD & refs/)",
  "dg.gitmap.l4s": "Mutable 41-byte branch pointer files",
  "dg.gitmap.x1": "git add: write blob + index",
  "dg.gitmap.x2": "git commit: write tree & commit",
  "dg.gitmap.x3": "Move branch pointer forward",
  "ch1.p3": "Once those four regions are clear, several quirks of Git make immediate sense. Why does a modified file already leave an object in `.git/objects/` right after `git add` before you even commit? Because `git add` already wrote the blob to the object store. Why does switching branches (`git checkout` / `git switch`) across thousands of files finish in milliseconds? Because Git only swaps working-tree paths whose hash differs from the target tree, without copying history. The next nine chapters trace every layer down from top to bottom.",
  "ch1.limit": "This chapter draws only the boundaries. The same objects on disk can live either as loose files or inside packed archives (chapter 8), and refs can use prefix-compressed binary tables instead of small text files (chapter 10).",
  "ch2.title": "Blobs & content addressing: from bytes to shard path",
  "toc.ch2": "Blobs & content addressing",
  "ch2.law": "Git never stores files by name: it prepends `&lt;type&gt; &lt;size&gt;\\0` to raw bytes, hashes the whole buffer with SHA-1 or SHA-256, and writes the zlib-compressed result under `.git/objects/&lt;first-two-hex&gt;/`.",
  "ch2.p1": "Drop two files with completely different names but identical bytes into the same repository and `.git/objects/` stores only <strong>one</strong> file object—a <strong>blob</strong> (binary large object). A blob carries no filename, no timestamp, and no permissions: nothing but raw content bytes.",
  "ch2.p2": "Specifically, when you run `git add` on `hello.txt` containing `hello git\\n` (10 bytes), Git constructs the exact ASCII prefix `blob 10\\0` and appends those 10 bytes, forming an 18-byte buffer. It hashes those 18 bytes: in a default SHA-1 repository the first 8 hex digits are always `8d0e4123` (40 hex chars / 20 bytes total), while in an `--object-format=sha256` repository the first 12 hex digits are `0cee7efe07c2` (64 hex chars / 32 bytes total). In parallel, <strong>zlib</strong> (RFC 1950/1951 DEFLATE) compresses those 18 bytes and stores them at `.git/objects/8d/...`. Why split the first two hex characters into up to 256 subdirectories? Because older filesystems (such as ext3 or HFS+) slow down markedly when hundreds of thousands of files sit in a single directory.",
  "dg.blob.cap": "Figure 2. How a loose blob object is built: `hello git\\n` gets an ASCII header to reach 18 bytes, is hashed for its address, compressed with zlib, and sharded into `.git/objects/`.",
  "dg.blob.aria": "Diagram: raw bytes gain a blob header, then split into hashing for the OID and zlib deflation, converging into the two-character shard path",
  "dg.blob.in": "Raw file bytes",
  "dg.blob.inv": "hello git\\n (10 B)",
  "dg.blob.prep": "prepend header",
  "dg.blob.hdr": "Uncompressed object (18 B)",
  "dg.blob.hdrv": "blob 10\\0hello git\\n",
  "dg.blob.hash": "SHA-1 / SHA-256",
  "dg.blob.oid": "8d0e4123...",
  "dg.blob.zlib": "zlib deflate",
  "dg.blob.zsz": "26 B compressed",
  "dg.blob.path": "Disk path (2-hex shard)",
  "dg.blob.pathv": ".git/objects/8d/0e4123...",
  "ch2.p3": "Type your own bytes into the lab below. Watch how flipping even <strong>one single bit</strong> in byte 0 flips roughly half of the output hash bits (<a href=\"../../systems/a-secret-in-public/#ch6\">the avalanche effect</a>) and lands the object in an entirely different shard folder—the foundation for Git guaranteeing bit-level integrity from a short hex ID.",
  "ch2.model.title": "Build a Git Blob Object Byte by Byte",
  "ch2.model.how": "Edit the text below, switch between SHA-1 and SHA-256, or tick \"Flip 1 bit\". The upper strip shows the exact <code>blob &lt;size&gt;\\0&lt;content&gt;</code> header bytes; the lower grid compares every bit of the hash before and after flipping 1 bit.",
