  "ch2.say.base": "With header prepended, total uncompressed size is {raw} bytes, compressed to {zlib} bytes by our pure-JS DEFLATE/zlib encoder; the leading two {algo} hex digits <code>{shard}</code> place it at <code>{path}</code>.",
  "ch2.say.flip": "Flipping just bit 0 of byte 0 inverted <em>{diff} of {bits} hash bits ({pct})</em> and jumped the shard directory from <code>{s0}</code> to <code>{s1}</code>—even a 1-bit difference never shares an object path.",
  "ch2.p4": "Note that the lab's `zlib` byte count comes from a pure-JS RFC 1951 fixed-Huffman DEFLATE encoder rewritten in `models.js`, which uses a few more bytes than native C zlib's dynamic Huffman tables on tiny strings; `tools/crosscheck.cjs` verifies that Node's `zlib.inflateSync` and real `git cat-file -p` decode its output byte-for-byte with identical SHA-1 and SHA-256 OIDs. Even an empty file (`0` bytes) gets `blob 0\\0` prepended, and an empty tree (`tree 0\\0`) in SHA-256 always hashes to `6ef19b41225c...`.",
  "ch2.limit": "The model leaves out: `core.compression` level tuning, symlinks (`120000`), and executable bit metadata (blobs never store permissions—that lives in the `tree` objects of the next chapter).",
  "ch3.title": "Trees & directory snapshots: why full snapshots stay small",
  "toc.ch3": "Trees & directory snapshots",
  "ch3.law": "Git encodes directories as `tree` objects containing sorted `&lt;mode&gt; &lt;name&gt;\\0&lt;20/32-byte raw hash&gt;` entries; editing one file creates new trees only along the path back to the root while reusing every unchanged file and subtree by hash.",
  "ch3.p1": "Because blobs contain neither filenames nor folder structure, Git needs a second object type to capture directories: the <strong>tree</strong>. Each folder becomes a compact binary record listing its immediate children: ASCII file mode (`100644` for regular files, `100755` for executables, `40000` for subdirectories—stored <strong>without</strong> a leading zero inside raw tree bytes, even though `git ls-tree` prints `040000`), an ASCII space, the entry name, a `\\0` NUL byte, and immediately the <strong>raw 20-byte (or 32-byte) binary hash</strong> rather than 40 hex characters.",
  "ch3.p2": "Tree entry sorting hides a subtle rule verified in `tools/crosscheck.cjs`: Git compares directory entries as if a trailing `'/'` (`0x2F`) were appended to every subdirectory name. If a directory contains both a file named `foo-bar.txt` and a subfolder named `foo`, `foo-bar.txt` sorts <strong>before</strong> `foo` because `'-'` (ASCII 45) is less than `'/'` (ASCII 47).",
  "dg.tree.cap": "Figure 3. Top: binary layout of one tree entry. Bottom: when only README changes between two commits, the new root tree points straight to the existing `src/` subtree hash without copying a single byte inside it.",
  "dg.tree.aria": "Diagram: binary mode, name, and raw OID fields of a tree entry, plus structural sharing of an unchanged subtree across two commits",
  "dg.tree.fmt": "Binary layout of one tree entry",
  "dg.tree.mode": "100644",
  "dg.tree.name": "hello.txt\\0",
  "dg.tree.oid": "20 B raw hash",
  "dg.tree.t1": "Commit 1 root",
  "dg.tree.t2": "Commit 2 root",
  "dg.tree.b1": "README v1",
  "dg.tree.b2": "README v2 (new)",
  "dg.tree.shared": "src/ subtree (reused)",
  "ch3.p3": "This is how Git saves \"a complete snapshot on every commit\" without multiplying disk usage. In a repository with $N$ files and tree depth $d$, modifying 1 file writes only <strong>1 new blob plus $d+1$ new tree objects</strong> along the ancestor path from that file to the root. Across 50 single-file commits in a 1,000-file repository, naive full-copy snapshots would accumulate 50,000 file copies, whereas Git's Merkle tree stores only 1,049 blobs.",
  "ch3.model.title": "Structural Sharing: Editing One File Touches Only One Branch",
  "ch3.model.how": "Pick which file to modify (top-level <code>README.md</code>, depth-1 <code>src/index.js</code>, or depth-2 <code>src/core/hash.js</code>), then vary file count <code>N</code> and commit count <code>C</code>. Left: our 10-file sample repo showing newly minted objects in gold and reused objects in green. Right: cumulative blobs across commits compared to naive copies.",
  "ch3.ctl.edit": "File edited",
