  "ch10.say": "At {n} refs, <code>packed-refs</code> takes {packSz} and <code>reftable</code> compresses to <em>{reftSz}</em>; {opDesc}. Across 100,000 files in 20 top-level dirs, checking out {act} active dirs shrinks the index to <em>{spEnt}</em> entries ({spSave} fewer).",
  "ch10.p2": "At 100,000 refs, `packed-refs` takes 7.18 MiB while `reftable` drops to 4.17 MiB—and the deeper win is avoiding rewriting all 7.18 MiB every time CI deletes a single tag. With cone-mode sparse checkout active on 2 of 20 top-level directories in a 100,000-file repo, `.git/index` collapses to 10,018 entries. <strong>Every version and roadmap claim in this chapter reflects November 2026</strong>—check newer `BreakingChanges` and forge announcements before relying on future defaults.",
  "ch10.limit": "The model leaves out: amortized compaction overhead under sustained high-frequency writes in `reftable`, and OS-specific filesystem inode/directory caching behavior across APFS, ext4, and NTFS.",
  "ch11.title": "Wrap-up: when `.git/` bloats, stalls, or loses commits, ask three questions",
  "toc.ch11": "Wrap-up: ask three questions",
  "ch11.law": "You never need to memorize every byte offset. When `.git/` blows up, `git status` crawls, or a mistake wipes commits, ask three questions: which objects are still reachable? Which cache did this operation miss? Which layer holds the pointer or delta?",
  "ch11.q1": "<strong>Why did the repo grow huge—and how do you actually shrink it?</strong> Check `git count-objects -vH` (`size-pack` vs `garbage`, chapter 8). If large binaries were committed in the past, deleting them in a new commit leaves old blobs intact in every ancestor commit; you have to rewrite history (chapters 3–4), expire reflogs (chapter 6), and run `git gc --prune=now`—or clone with `--filter=blob:none` (chapter 9).",
  "ch11.q2": "<strong>Why did `git status` or branch commands suddenly turn sluggish?</strong> Check if `.git/index` `stat` caches invalidated en masse (container volume mounts changing `uid`/`mtime`) or if an un-sparsed monorepo needs cone mode (`sparseIndex`, chapters 5 and 10). Slow branch listing or deletion across tens of thousands of refs points to `files` directory scans or full `packed-refs` rewrites—test `git refs migrate --ref-format=reftable` (chapter 10).",
  "ch11.q3": "<strong>Can a botched `reset` or `rebase` still be recovered?</strong> As long as you ran <strong>`git add` or `git commit`</strong>, those objects entered `.git/objects/` (chapters 1–2): commits dropped by `git reset --hard`, bad rebases, or force-deleted branches remain pinned locally by `.git/logs/HEAD` for 30 days by default via `git reflog` (chapters 4 and 6). The <strong>only</strong> work Git cannot rescue is working-tree edits that never even ran `git add`.",
  "ch11.sum": "In one sentence: `git add` and `git commit` build an immutable <strong>content-addressed tree (blob → tree → commit)</strong> and quietly nudge a 41-byte branch sticky note forward—giving you instantaneous snapshots, lightweight branching, on-demand diffs, delta-packed storage, and weeks of local safety nets.",
  "ch11.links": "Related essays: <a href=\"../../systems/a-secret-in-public/#ch6\">A Secret Agreed in Public, Chapter 6</a> (one-way hashes and SHA-256 avalanche), <a href=\"../../algorithms/graph-algorithms/#step0\">Graph Algorithms</a> (graphs and ancestry walks), <a href=\"../../systems/what-a-drive-is-made-of/#ch6\">What a Drive Is Made Of, Chapter 6</a> (why flash FTLs and object stores append new copies and remap pointers rather than overwriting in place), and W1 of this series, <a href=\"../how-threejs-draws-a-scene/\">How Three.js Draws a Scene</a>.",
  "footer": "<p><strong>Sources.</strong> All object formats, protocols, and release behaviors match <strong>Git 2.56.0</strong> (`/opt/homebrew/bin/git`, released 28 September 2026) and its shipped primary documentation: `gitformat-loose(5)`, `gitformat-index(5)`, `gitformat-pack(5)`, `gitformat-signature(5)`, `gitprotocol-v2(5)`, `gitrepository-layout(5)`, `git-refs(1)`, `BreakingChanges.adoc`, `RelNotes/2.45.0.adoc`–`2.56.0.adoc`, and under `technical/`: `racy-git.adoc`, `pack-heuristics.adoc`, `reftable.adoc`, `hash-function-transition.adoc`, `partial-clone.adoc`, `sparse-checkout.adoc`, `sparse-index.adoc`, and `scalar.adoc`. Algorithms and wire standards: Eugene W. Myers, <em>An O(ND) Difference Algorithm and Its Variations</em>, Algorithmica 1(2), 1986; RFC 1950 (zlib) and RFC 1951 (DEFLATE); RFC 3174 (SHA-1) and NIST FIPS 180-4 (SHA-256). File-by-file references and exact crosscheck logs are recorded in `BRIEF.md` in this directory.</p><p><strong>The models.</strong> The page runs zero external libraries and executes no native Git binary in the browser. `models.js` implements pure-JS SHA-1, SHA-256, CRC32, Adler-32 + DEFLATE/zlib compression, loose object framing, tree entry sorting and structural sharing, commit/tag serialization, `DIRC` v2 index encoding and `stat`-cache / Racy-Git evaluation, Myers diff and 3-way merge, `PACK` v2 varint and `OFS_DELTA` / `COPY` / `INSERT` bytecode encoding/decoding, `.idx` v2 fanout binary search, and clone/ref/sparse scaling models. `tools/crosscheck.cjs` verified 58 real outputs directly against `/opt/homebrew/bin/git` (2.56.0) in a scratch directory—blobs, nested trees, commits, tags, index checksums, Racy-Git zeroed size, five 3-way merges, real `.pack` decompression and `OFS_DELTA` reconstruction, and `reftable` headers—achieving 58/58 exact agreement. Written with Antigravity.</p>",
